---

# URL Driven State

Filtering

Sorting

Pagination

Search

should be reflected in the URL.

Example

/dashboard?page=2

/products?category=laptop

/search?q=iphone

The server should render the correct result.

Do not fetch after changing local state.

---

# Data Ownership

Server owns:

Business data

CMS

Database

API responses

Authentication

Permissions

Client owns:

Open modal

Expanded sidebar

Current tab

Hovered item

Input values

Animations

---

# Loading UI

Loading UI should only exist for:

Streaming

Slow nested routes

Lazy imports

Never because data is fetched in useEffect.

---

# Folder Structure

lib/

api/

users.ts

dashboard.ts

analytics.ts

posts.ts

comments.ts

auth.ts

cache.ts

types.ts

Every file exports typed functions.

No component should know endpoint URLs.

---

# Error Handling

Every fetch helper should:

Validate response

Throw typed errors

Never return undefined

Example

```ts
if (!response.ok) {
   throw new Error("Failed to fetch dashboard");
}
```

---

# Authentication

Read session on the server.

Never fetch user information after hydration if the page depends on it.

---

# Performance Rules

✅ Server Components first

✅ Native fetch

✅ Promise.all

✅ Cache intentionally

✅ Revalidate intentionally

✅ Layout owns shared data

✅ URL-driven state

✅ Client only for interaction

---

# Anti Patterns

❌ useEffect fetching page content

❌ Axios everywhere

❌ Duplicate API calls

❌ Client fetching user/session

❌ Nested sequential fetches

❌ Passing URLs into components

❌ Business logic inside UI

❌ Fetching Navbar/Footer repeatedly

❌ Loading spinners for normal pages

---

# Code Review Checklist

Before merging, verify:

[ ] Is this page a Server Component?

[ ] Is page data fetched on the server?

[ ] Is fetch centralized?

[ ] Is caching intentional?

[ ] Is Promise.all used?

[ ] Is shared data fetched in layout?

[ ] Is this client fetch actually necessary?

[ ] Can this become a Server Component?

[ ] Can duplicate requests be removed?

[ ] Does first HTML already contain all important content?

---

# Ultimate Principle

The browser should receive a fully-rendered page.

Client-side fetching is the exception.

Server-side fetching is the default.

Design every page so that the first paint already contains meaningful content.

If a user can see a loading spinner because the browser is waiting for data, reconsider the architecture.